    "start_byte": 2793,
    "text": "theorem tendsto_transportedFlag_orbit_zero (family : RepresentativeShellFamily)\n    (i : MathlibAnnex.CStarAlgebra.PureState.GNSClass Limit)\n    (σ : Representation Limit H) (ξ : H)\n    (hξ : ∀ a, Representation.vectorFunctional σ ξ a = trace a) (b : Limit) :\n    Tendsto (fun n ↦ σ (transportedFlag family i n) (σ b ξ)) atTop (nhds 0)"
  },
  "content": {
    "assumptions": "Let $A$ be the completed CAR algebra with matrix stages $M_{2^n}(\\mathbb C)$ embedded by $a\\mapsto a\\otimes I_2$. Write $f_n$ for the image of the first diagonal matrix unit, $p_n=f_n-f_{n+1}$, and $\\omega$ for the root state characterized by $\\omega(\\iota_n(a))=a_{00}$ on each stage, where $\\iota_n$ is its canonical embedding.\n\nIndex chosen pure-state Gelfand-Naimark-Segal (GNS) representations by their unitary-equivalence classes $j\\in I$, with root index $o$ and representative states $\\varphi_j$.\n\nA fixed shell family consists of complex-linear unital star automorphisms $\\alpha_j$ and elements $w_{j,n}\\in A$ satisfying $\\varphi_j(\\alpha_j(a))=\\omega(a)$, $w_{j,n}^*w_{j,n}=\\alpha_j(p_n)$ and $w_{j,n}w_{j,n}^*=p_n$.\n\nAt the root, $\\alpha_o$ is the identity and $w_{o,n}=p_n$. Set $f_{j,n}=\\alpha_j(f_n)$.\n\nThe space $K$ lies in an arbitrary universe; it need not be separable. The vector-functional equality is assumed for every $a\\in A$. Cyclicity of $\\xi$ in all of $K$ is not assumed, nor is irreducibility of $\\sigma$. Inner products are linear in their second argument. Here $B(K)$ is the algebra of bounded complex-linear operators on $K$.",
    "conclusion": "Each fixed vector $\\sigma(b)\\xi$ is sent to zero in the limit. The estimate $\\|\\sigma(f_{j,n})\\sigma(b)\\xi\\|^2\\leq\\|b\\|^2 2^{-n}$ proves this directly.",
    "content_completeness": "CARD_CONTENT_COMPLETE",
    "definition_explanation": null,
    "display_title": "Transported flags vanish on vectors generated by a trace vector",
    "language_tag": "en",
    "lean_realization_notes": "This declaration is a statement about orbit vectors and norm convergence of their images. It does not claim operator-norm convergence of the projections, or convergence on every vector of an arbitrary ambient representation without an additional argument.",
    "natural_language_statement": "Let $\\sigma:A\\to B(K)$ be a unital star representation of the completed CAR algebra on a complete complex Hilbert space. Suppose $\\xi\\in K$ implements the normalized trace from [the CAR trace construction](https://github.com/r-tanaka-math/mathlib-annex/blob/437e6e46228bbb8e91211ebded349d7a30020e73/MathlibAnnex/Analysis/CStarAlgebra/CAR/Trace.lean#L62-L64): $\\langle\\xi,\\sigma(a)\\xi\\rangle=\\tau(a)$ for all $a\\in A$. For the transported projections $f_{j,n}$ of a fixed shell family and every $b\\in A$, one has $\\sigma(f_{j,n})\\sigma(b)\\xi\\to0$ in norm as $n\\to\\infty$.",
    "one_sentence_role": "Turns decay of projection traces into norm convergence on each source-orbit vector.",
    "proof_steps": [
      {
        "step_id": "s1",
        "text": "Use $p^*=p=p^2$ and star preservation to express the squared norm as $\\tau(b^*pb)$."
      },
      {
        "step_id": "s2",
        "text": "Use the trace identity and the positive order bound on $pbb^*p$ to obtain $\\|\\sigma(p)\\sigma(b)\\xi\\|^2\\leq\\|b\\|^2\\tau(p)$."
      },
      {
        "step_id": "s3",
        "text": "Insert the exact value $\\tau(f_{j,n})=2^{-n}$. The nonnegative squared norms are squeezed to zero, which implies norm convergence of the vectors."
      }
    ],
    "proof_summary": "For a projection $p$, the vector-functional hypothesis gives $\\|\\sigma(p)\\sigma(b)\\xi\\|^2=\\tau(b^*pb)$. Traciality rewrites this as $\\tau(pbb^*p)$. Since $0\\leq pbb^*p\\leq\\|b\\|^2p$, positivity gives the bound $\\|b\\|^2\\tau(p)$. Apply it to $p=f_{j,n}$, whose trace is $2^{-n}$ by [the transported-flag trace theorem](https://github.com/r-tanaka-math/mathlib-annex/blob/437e6e46228bbb8e91211ebded349d7a30020e73/MathlibAnnex/Analysis/CStarAlgebra/CAR/TraceFlag.lean#L42-L46), and let $n$ tend to infinity."
  },
